(area "ui" :when "the agent is about to show the user something: a table, form, buttons, chart, view or panel, or a view the user clicked just came back, or the user asks about the app itself: what a part of it is, where something is, how to do something in it, or to open one of its pages")

(memory "internal/ui/reactive" :when "a view needs a dropdown, toggle or filter that updates it at once, without asking the agent again"
  "Reactive controls: :on-change on ui/select or ui/checkbox. One-argument
function, run when user picks or ticks. No submit, no model turn. Gets same
alist as form handler, answers by rendering:

(defun panel (window)
  (ui/stack
    (ui/select '(\"7\" \"30\" \"90\") :name \"d\" :value window
      :on-change (lambda (values) (ui/render (panel (cdr (assoc \"d\" values))))))
    (ui/table (issues-since window))))
(ui/render (panel \"7\"))

Text fields have no :on-change on purpose: each keystroke would round-trip,
redrawing the field being typed in. Put ui/input in ui/form, let submit
carry it.

Button INSIDE a form gets the same alist, if its action takes one argument.")

(memory "internal/ui/actions" :when "a button in a view must keep working after a redraw, or an old view's controls must be dropped, or a click just reached a stale view"
  (format nil "Actions close over their environment. REPL persistent, so
(setq counter (+ counter 1)) in a handler sticks; a later step reads it like
any global. They die with the REPL: clearing conversation drops every action.

Action may be a quoted call to a global function instead: '(refresh-dashboard)
or '(set-filter \"open\") . Args passed as written, not evaluated. Field alist
appended when the function takes one more argument. Function looked up by name
on click, so redefine it to change what click does. Never dropped past ~d
handlers, kept across REPL restart. Prefer it for long-lived views:

(defun set-filter (f) (ui/render (panel f)))
(ui/button \"open only\" '(set-filter \"open\"))

(ui/forget view) drops actions behind view's controls; view shows expired,
clicks run nothing. Use on a view you replaced or closed. Returns count
dropped." *ui-max-handlers*))

(memory "internal/ui/send" :when "a view needs a control that hands a request back to the agent along with what the user entered"
  "(ui/send text...) arguments join as echo joins them. The result joins
conversation as user message, so you take the next turn and answer normally:

(ui/form (lambda (values) (ui/send \"search the issues for\" (cdr (assoc \"q\" values))))
  (ui/input :name \"q\" :label \"search\")
  :submit \"ask\")

Send for judgement, handle in Lisp for work. Prose request, or a decision or
step with no code written, needs a turn. Filtering, paging, recomputing, calling
a loaded tool is free. Handler may render AND send: render what user sees while
you think, then send. Each send in one handler is own message: one click, one
thing, so send once. Only handlers deliver: ui/send in your own step does nothing,
you already take a turn. Write a request, \"search the issues for auth\", not a
note, \"user clicked search\".")

(memory "internal/ui/panels" :when "a file or view must open beside the chat, a tab must be shown, moved, focused or closed, or the trace shown or hidden, or a sidebar opened or closed"
  (format nil "Panel beside chat: tabs in splits, any number across, up to ~d
  stacked down. Folder is one of ~{~s~^ ~}. :split is one of ~{~s~^ ~};
  ~s (default) adds tab to focused split, others open new split
  beside it, across or down.
- (ui/open-file folder name [:split ~s ]) opens file preview in tab.
  List folder first: unknown name opens tab saying so. Open file gets focus,
  no second tab. Returns tab id.
- (ui/open-view title view [:split ~s ]) widget in tab, not under message.
  Controls run in this REPL as usual. Same title replaces that tab. Returns
  tab id.
- (ui/close tab) · (ui/focus tab) by id open returned. ui/close gives t if tab
  was open, nil if not.
- (ui/trace) shows this chat's trace beside it, (ui/trace nil) hides it, as
  /trace would; hide also closes trace tab of this chat. t if chat open in
  panel, nil if not.
- (ui/sidebar \"left\") opens left sidebar, (ui/sidebar \"right\" nil) closes
  right one, as /sidebar and /right would. t if now open, nil if closed.
- (ui/tabs) lists panel tabs: (:id id :kind :file|:view|:chat|:trace|:page :title
  title :shown t :focused t) . User opens, closes, moves tabs too: read it
  before closing or focusing by id from earlier step.
- (ui/file-link text folder name) widget; click opens file in tab. List
  files user can open:

(ui/render (ui/stack (ui/file-link \"Q3 report\" ~s \"q3.pdf\")
    (ui/file-link \"photo\" ~s \"cat.png\")))

Open for user when they ask to see file; link when offering several."
    *ui-max-splits* *ui-folders* *ui-splits* *ui-default-split*
    (car (cdr *ui-splits*)) (car (cdr (cdr *ui-splits*)))
    (car (cdr *ui-folders*)) (car *ui-folders*)))

(memory "internal/ui/build" :when "the agent is about to build a visual view, dashboard, chart or interactive page"
  "Build ui view and test it without rendering. ui/render only once it works.")

(memory "internal/ui/basics" :when "the agent is about to show a table, form or buttons in chat, or any view the user can click or type into"
  "Example panel, a form whose action takes the alist, and a button whose action takes none:

(defun panel ()
  (ui/stack
    (ui/heading \"Open issues\")
    (ui/table (head issues-1 10))
    (ui/form (lambda (values) (ui/render (results (cdr (assoc \"q\" values)))))
      (ui/input :name \"q\" :label \"filter\" :placeholder \"auth\")
      :submit \"search\")))
(ui/render (panel))

**Actions** are ordinary Lisp functions. Button or form action runs IN THIS
REPL on use, NO model turn between: click, closure runs, view replaced, you
are not asked. So every branch you anticipate is free.
- Button action takes no argument: (lambda () (ui/render (panel))) . Form
  action takes one: alist of every enclosed field's :name and contents, read
  with assoc.
- Action answers via ui/render, replacing view in place; rendering nothing
  leaves view as is.
- Field contents always strings, except ui/checkbox: real boolean, so
(if (cdr (assoc \"o\" values)) ...) works. (read s) turns string into number.
  \"\" and \"false\" are both TRUE, hence boolean tick box.
- Render to show: a table or text to read, something to DO, a filter to change,
  a row to act on, a choice to make. Long data: write a file and open it beside
  chat. Recall memory internal/ui/panels for how.
- You get only a summary, as in \"rendered stack, 8 elements, 3 actions\";
  widget is never sent back, since describing it would spend the context
  drawing it saved.")

(memory "internal/ui/showing" :when "the agent is about to show the user something, or is tempted to reply with a talking form"
  "Echo or print comes back to you alone. What else reaches user besides final
prose is set by each extension's section of reference below: what it shows
user, and how. User cannot reply mid-loop: \"user\" between steps is REPL handing
back result, so no question you ask gets answered. Showing user what they asked
for is real work. Talking is not. NEVER emit form only to chat or keep loop
alive. `(identity \"Ready for instructions.\") `, `(identity \"Waiting for your command.\") `
and the like are worst failure mode: compute nothing, each buys another step of
talking to yourself until step cap. Every form must compute, inspect, define,
render or call a tool.
Unrendered widget shows nothing. Put what user should read inside a widget,
never in an echo. End with short prose: point at what you rendered, do not
repeat it, e.g. \"the 27 open issues are in the table above\".")

(memory "internal/ui/pages" :when "a page of the app itself must open beside the chat, such as its settings, or the user asks to see one"
  (format nil "(ui/open page [:split ~s ]) opens app page in tab beside chat, as user
would from sidebar. Pages, nothing else exists:
~{- ~a: ~a~%~}Open page already focused, no second tab. Returns tab id, as in \"page:~a\" .

User asks to open page: never bare ui/open. ui/point its anchor with :open
first, so user learns where it lives, then sees it. Recall memory
internal/ui/point for how."
    (car (cdr *ui-splits*)) (apply append *ui-pages*) (car (car *ui-pages*))))

(memory "internal/ui/point" :when "the user asks where something is in the app or how to reach it, such as where to upload a file, start a chat or change a setting, asks to open one of its pages, or should be shown a slash command"
  (format nil "Point at part of app itself, not a widget.
(ui/point anchor [:open page] [:fill text] [:mark ~s ] [:size ~s ] [:click t])
puts pointer on anchor few seconds, no overlay, no text. Say what it is in
your reply. Returns anchor.
- anchor is an id from memory internal/ui/anchors-SECTION , SECTION one of
  ~{~a~^ ~}. Nothing else exists. Section not loaded: recall the one you need.
- :mark one of ~{~a~^ ~}. ~a (default) points at it; oval circles it.
- :size one of ~{~a~^ ~}, ~a default: pointer size, or how loose oval sits.
- :click t shows pointer clicking anchor.
- :open page: pointer clicks anchor, then page opens beside chat, as if user
  clicked it. Pages: ~{~a~^ ~}.
- Anchor inside trace: (ui/trace) first, hidden till then.
- :fill only on ~s: text lands in message box after pointer, user sends it.
  Show power user a slash command by filling it, never by running it:
(ui/point ~s :fill \"/trace\")

User asks where thing is, or asks to open page: ui/point its anchor with :open
first, never bare ui/open. User learns where it lives, then sees it. Reply
\"Account settings live here, gear at bottom of sidebar.\" with
(ui/point \"account\" :open \"account\")

Every part of app has anchor. NEVER tell user you do not know where it is,
NEVER guess in words: point at it.
Point when user asks where thing is; tour when user asks how to do something
in app, recall memory internal/ui/tour . Neither for chat content."
    (car (cdr *ui-marks*)) (car *ui-mark-sizes*) *ui-anchor-sections*
    *ui-marks* *ui-default-mark* *ui-mark-sizes* *ui-default-mark-size*
    (mapcar car *ui-pages*) *ui-fill-anchor* *ui-fill-anchor*))

(memory "internal/ui/tour" :when "the user asks how to do something in the app, such as upload or attach a file, start or find a chat, or change a setting, or should be walked through part of it step by step"
  (format nil "(ui/tour id step... [:next \"next\" :done \"done\" :skip \"skip\"]) walks user
through app, one step at a time, at most ~d steps. Step is plist
(:anchor anchor :page page :say text :until ~{~a~^|~}) .
- :say is text of callout. No :anchor: callout in middle.
- :anchor is id from memory internal/ui/anchors-SECTION , SECTION one of
  ~{~a~^ ~}. Section not loaded: recall the one you need. Anchor in closed folder or page: app reveals it first.
- :page opens that page beside chat as step starts, so anchor inside it is on
  screen. Pages: ~{~a~^ ~}.
- :until ~a (default) waits for next button; ~a pulses circle on anchor and
  also advances on click of it, needs :anchor. Next button always there.
- :next :done :skip name buttons. One-step tour shows only :done, \"got it\"
  unless named; :skip only when given.

(ui/tour \"upload-files\"
  '(:anchor \"knowledge/upload\" :say \"Drop PDF here\" :until click)
  '(:say \"Now ask me about it\"))

New tour replaces one on screen. User may skip. Finish or skip sends you
message, \"I finished the upload-files tour.\", so you take next turn. New user
message pauses tour; tours expire on their own.

(ui/tour-done? id) t if user finished tour before, any chat. Check before
offering, do not replay finished tour unasked."
    *ui-tour-max-steps* *ui-tour-untils* *ui-anchor-sections*
    (mapcar car *ui-pages*) (car *ui-tour-untils*) (car (cdr *ui-tour-untils*))))

(memory "internal/ui/app" :when "the user asks what a part of the app is or how the app is laid out: sidebars, folders, panel, tabs, bottom bar, workspaces or pages"
  (format nil "App user sees, around this chat:
- Left sidebar: workspace name top left switches workspace, + beside it makes
  new one. Then home, new chat, folders: ~{~a~^ ~}. Entries README.md,
  settings, preferences open pages. Gear at bottom: account. User drags
  entries, also across to right sidebar; hides them in preferences.
- Right sidebar: closed by default, opened from right edge. Holds whatever
  entries user moved there.
- Panel, middle: tabs in splits. Chat is a tab too. Files, views, pages, a
  chat's trace open as tabs. Tab x closes. Pane menu splits right or down,
  flips split, maximizes, closes pane. Handles between panes resize.
- Bottom bar: one button per pane to jump to it, zoom buttons and desktop app
  status on desktop. Slots user rearranges in preferences.
- Pages: ~{~a~^ ~}. Recall memory internal/ui/pages for what each holds.
Chat itself: recall memory internal/ui/chat . Slash commands:
internal/ui/commands . Pointing at any of it: internal/ui/point ."
    *ui-sidebar-folders* (mapcar car *ui-pages*)))

(memory "internal/ui/chat" :when "the user asks how the chat works: the message box, sending, stopping, queueing, attaching, the details under an answer, feedback, the todo, sub-agent threads or the trace"
  "Message box at bottom of chat:
- Enter sends, shift+enter new line. While you work, send turns to stop;
  text typed then queues as next message, shown above box, x removes it.
  Files wait until your reply ends.
- Attach button adds files; chips show above box, x removes one.
- / alone in box opens command menu, recall memory internal/ui/commands .
  @ anywhere mentions a file from knowledge or attachments, or a sub-agent.
- When you ask with fixed choices, choices replace box. Keys 1-9 pick;
  type instead goes back to box.
Under each answer:
- Line of details: time, duration, steps, input, cached and output tokens,
  model, provider, show in trace. Which show is a preference.
- Helpful and not helpful buttons, then optional text. Long message folds
  behind read more.
- Todo card when you keep one; click on header folds it to a count.
Sub-agent threads: strip at bottom left of chat; their steps also show
inside trace.
Trace: every step of a turn, your prose and code, results, ui, memories
recalled, asks, thinking. Opens beside chat on click of agent message, show
in trace, or /trace. Find box, and toggles for what it shows. Tell user to
read it when they ask why you did something. You open it with (ui/trace) ,
close with (ui/trace nil) .")

(memory "internal/ui/commands" :when "the user asks what slash commands exist, how to do something a command does, or the agent would point the user at one"
  (format nil "User types / in message box for commands. They run in app, not in this
REPL: you cannot run one, only show it with
(ui/point ~s :fill \"/name\") .
~{~{- ~a: ~a~{, alias ~a~}~}~%~}Desktop screen only: ~{~a~^ ~}."
    *ui-fill-anchor* *ui-commands* *ui-desktop-commands*))
